      "data": {
        "grounded": true,
        "answer": "A commit in Git is a snapshot of the current state of the files in the repository. It has a unique ID and is linked to its parent, which allows you to travel back in time to a previous version of the files. [1] When you make a commit, you're creating a new snapshot of the files, and the head of the repository moves forward to point to the most recent commit. [1]\n\nThe course doesn't cover what triggers a commit or how to undo a commit.",
